    3. Build wire network connecting top endpoints to bottom endpoints implementing permutation P. Use manhattan routing with vertical column segments and horizontal crossover segments. At each perpendicular wire crossing, place a pair of color-5 cells on the through-wire flanking the intersection. Embed 0-3 color-9 rectangular blocks (2x2 to 5x4) along wire segments: pick a contiguous rectangle of existing wire cells (color 8) that covers 1-3 parallel wires, replace those cells with color 9. Each wire entering the rect must exit from the opposite face (straight pass-through). Exception: if the rect has exactly 1 entry + 1 exit on adjacent faces, the wire turns inside.
    4. Derive input from output -- for each channel, randomly choose which side gets the input token. Most tokens are solid (reverses diamond to solid at that position). Pick 0-2 channels to have X input token instead; for X channels, far-end is usually left empty (~67%) or has pre-placed diamond (~33%). Remaining non-X far-end positions are left empty (bg).
    5. Oracle -- run solver, verify output matches expected.

